---
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>3.5 — De Morgan's Laws &amp; The DungeonGatekeeper Build</h1>

<p>
    <strong>CED Topics 2.2, 2.5, 2.6 · 90-Minute Design-First Build.</strong> Compound boolean
    expressions and De Morgan's Laws appear on the AP CS A exam every single year—both in Multiple
    Choice ("Which expression is equivalent to...") and in Free Response guard conditions. Today you
    design <code>DungeonGatekeeper.java</code> by reasoning through truth tables, interval negation,
    operator precedence, and short-circuit shields <em>before</em> unlocking the test suite.
</p>

<div class="callout">
    <strong>Design-First Workflow (One-Shot Challenge):</strong>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <a href="../data/DungeonGatekeeper.java" download>DungeonGatekeeper.java</a> — starter
            file with Javadocs, stubs, and a built-in <strong>Dungeon Gatekeeper Security
            Bench</strong> in <code>main()</code>.
        </li>
    </ul>
    <p style="margin-top:0.5rem; margin-bottom:0.25rem;">
        <strong>How to work today:</strong>
    </p>
    <ol style="margin-bottom:0.5rem;">
        <li><strong>Design before coding:</strong> Answer the Guided Design questions for each
            gatekeeper rule so you catch De Morgan, boundary flip, precedence, and divide-by-zero
            traps on paper first.</li>
        <li><strong>Draft &amp; test in the sandbox:</strong> Draft each method below, click
            <strong>Copy</strong> to paste into <code>DungeonGatekeeper.java</code>, and test your
            rules with:</li>
    </ol>
    <pre><code>javac DungeonGatekeeper.java
java DungeonGatekeeper</code></pre>
    <p style="margin-top:0.4rem; margin-bottom:0;">
        <strong>Step 3 (One-Shot Pass):</strong> Unlock <code>DungeonGatekeeperCheck.java</code>
        below and aim to pass all <strong>31 checks</strong> on your very first run.
    </p>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 1 · De Morgan's Laws</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 1: <code>isVaultLockedOut(boolean hasRunekey, boolean isBloodMoon)</code></h3>

    <div class="design-context">
        <h4>What This Function Does &amp; Why It Exists</h4>
        <p>
            The Shadow Vault opens only when a hero holds the Runekey <strong>AND</strong> the Blood
            Moon is rising (<code>hasRunekey &amp;&amp; isBloodMoon</code>). The security guard needs
            the exact opposite condition—returning <code>true</code> whenever the vault stays locked,
            written <strong>without</strong> an outer <code>!</code> around parentheses.
        </p>
        <h4>Parameters &amp; Return Value</h4>
        <ul class="param-list">
            <li><code>boolean hasRunekey</code>, <code>boolean isBloodMoon</code> — unlock flags.</li>
            <li><strong>Returns (<code>boolean</code>):</strong> De Morgan equivalent of
                <code>!(hasRunekey &amp;&amp; isBloodMoon)</code> with no outer <code>!</code>.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Guided Program Design: <code>isVaultLockedOut</code></h3>
        <p>Apply De Morgan's Law instead of algebra intuition:</p>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>De Morgan Truth Table:</strong> Why does distributing <code>!</code> like
                algebra to get <code>!hasRunekey &amp;&amp; !isBloodMoon</code> fail when
                <code>(true, false)</code> or <code>(false, true)</code>? What happens to
                <code>&amp;&amp;</code> and <code>||</code> when you push <code>!</code> inside
                parentheses?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Draft &amp; Test in <code>DungeonGatekeeper.java</code></h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Write your one-line <code>isVaultLockedOut</code> method body, copy it into
                <code>DungeonGatekeeper.java</code>, and check Part 1 in
                <code>java DungeonGatekeeper</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 2 · Interval Negation &amp; Boundary Flips</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 2: <code>isSafeStatScore(int score)</code> &amp; <code>isInvalidStatScore(int score)</code></h3>

    <div class="design-context">
        <h4>What These Functions Do &amp; Why They Exist</h4>
        <p>
            A legal 5e ability score rolled on 3d6 lies in the closed interval <code>[3, 18]</code>
            (<code>score &gt;= 3 &amp;&amp; score &lt;= 18</code>). <code>isSafeStatScore</code>
            confirms a score is inside <code>[3, 18]</code>, while <code>isInvalidStatScore</code>
            detects scores outside <code>[3, 18]</code>—written cleanly without <code>!</code>.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Guided Program Design: Interval Negation</h3>
        <p>Catch the two interval bugs before coding:</p>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Impossible Number &amp; Boundary Thief:</strong> Why does
                <code>score &lt; 3 &amp;&amp; score &gt; 18</code> return <code>false</code> for
                <em>every</em> integer, and why does <code>score &lt;= 3 || score &gt;= 18</code>
                wrongly reject <code>3</code> and <code>18</code>? What is the exact negation of
                <code>&gt;=</code> and <code>&lt;=</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Draft &amp; Test in <code>DungeonGatekeeper.java</code></h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Write both <code>isSafeStatScore</code> and <code>isInvalidStatScore</code>, copy them
                into <code>DungeonGatekeeper.java</code>, and verify scores <code>2, 3, 10, 18, 19</code>
                in <code>java DungeonGatekeeper</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 3 · Boolean Operator Precedence</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 3: <code>doesBossEnrage(boolean lowHp, boolean minionFell, boolean isSilenced)</code></h3>

    <div class="design-context">
        <h4>What This Function Does &amp; Why It Exists</h4>
        <p>
            <strong>Rule:</strong> The boss enrages if its HP is low <strong>OR</strong> a minion
            fell this round—<strong>provided the boss is NOT silenced</strong>. If the boss is
            silenced, it can never enrage.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Guided Program Design: <code>doesBossEnrage</code></h3>
        <p>Check Java's precedence order before translating English to code:</p>
        <ol>
            <li><strong><code>&amp;&amp;</code> vs. <code>||</code> Precedence:</strong> Just like
                <code>*</code> binds tighter than <code>+</code>, <code>&amp;&amp;</code> binds
                tighter than <code>||</code>. Why does omitting parentheses in
                <code>lowHp || minionFell &amp;&amp; !isSilenced</code> wrongly return
                <code>true</code> on <code>(true, false, true)</code>, and where must parentheses go?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Draft &amp; Test in <code>DungeonGatekeeper.java</code></h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Write your <code>doesBossEnrage</code> method body, copy it into
                <code>DungeonGatekeeper.java</code>, and check Part 3 in
                <code>java DungeonGatekeeper</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity expand-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 4 · Short-Circuit Guards</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 4: <code>hasFairGoldSplit(int partySize, int totalGold, int minShare)</code></h3>

    <div class="design-context">
        <h4>What This Function Does &amp; Why It Exists</h4>
        <p>
            Looted gold is fair only when: (1) <code>partySize &gt; 0</code>, (2) integer share
            <code>totalGold / partySize &gt;= minShare</code>, and (3) zero coins remain
            (<code>totalGold % partySize == 0</code>).
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Guided Program Design: <code>hasFairGoldSplit</code></h3>
        <p>Order your <code>&amp;&amp;</code> clauses safely:</p>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Short-Circuit Divide-by-Zero Guard:</strong> What exception crashes your
                program if you check <code>totalGold / partySize &gt;= minShare</code> <em>before</em>
                <code>partySize &gt; 0</code> when <code>partySize == 0</code>, and how does
                short-circuit evaluation prevent the crash when <code>partySize &gt; 0</code> comes
                first?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Draft &amp; Test in <code>DungeonGatekeeper.java</code></h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Write your <code>hasFairGoldSplit</code> method body, copy it into
                <code>DungeonGatekeeper.java</code>, and check <code>partySize = 0</code> in
                <code>java DungeonGatekeeper</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity rpg">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Part 5 · AP Synthesis: Multi-Gate Zone Classifier</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <h3>Part 5: <code>classifyEncounterZone(int floor, int partyLevel, boolean hasTorch, String guildRank)</code></h3>

    <div class="design-context">
        <h4>Priority Table</h4>
        <table>
            <tr><th>Priority</th><th>Condition</th><th>Return</th></tr>
            <tr><td>1</td><td><code>floor &lt;= 0 || partyLevel &lt;= 0 || guildRank == null || guildRank.equals("Banned")</code></td><td><code>-1</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td>2</td><td><code>floor &gt;= 10 &amp;&amp; (partyLevel &gt;= 12 || guildRank.equals("Grandmaster")) &amp;&amp; hasTorch</code></td><td><code>3</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td>3</td><td><code>!hasTorch &amp;&amp; !(floor &lt; 5 &amp;&amp; guildRank.equals("Veteran"))</code></td><td><code>2</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td>4</td><td>All other valid parties</td><td><code>1</code></td></tr>
        </table>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Guided Program Design: <code>classifyEncounterZone</code></h3>
        <p>Simplify Priority 3 using De Morgan's Law and check null safety:</p>
        <ol>
            <li><strong>Simplifying Priority 3 &amp; Null Safety:</strong> Rewrite
                <code>!(floor &lt; 5 &amp;&amp; guildRank.equals("Veteran"))</code> without an
                outer <code>!</code> using De Morgan's Law, and explain why checking
                <code>guildRank == null</code> in Priority 1 protects Priorities 2 and 3 from
                <code>NullPointerException</code>?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Draft &amp; Test in <code>DungeonGatekeeper.java</code></h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Write your complete <code>classifyEncounterZone</code> method body, copy it into
                <code>DungeonGatekeeper.java</code>, and test all parts in
                <code>java DungeonGatekeeper</code> before unlocking
                <code>DungeonGatekeeperCheck.java</code> below?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Done When</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>All six methods run without crashing in <code>java DungeonGatekeeper</code></li>
            <li>You unlock <code>DungeonGatekeeperCheck.java</code> below and aim for <strong><code>31 passed, 0 failed</code></strong> on your first run</li>
            <li>You have downloaded your <code>.json</code> answers file for Canvas</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-03/de-morgan.html">De Morgan's Laws</a> — truth tables, range negations, precedence</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-03/boolean-logic.html">Boolean Logic</a> — short-circuit evaluation</li>
    </ul>
</div>`;
---
<Lesson id="3.5" title="3.5 De Morgan's Laws &amp; The DungeonGatekeeper Build — AP CS A" root=".." css="../style.css">
  <Fragment set:html={body} />
</Lesson>
